## Server-Side Code & Execution Exploits (Critical Impact)
These vulnerabilities occur when backend servers or automated systems process user-submitted comment strings as active instructions rather than plain text.

* 1. OS Command Injection via Comments (CWE-78)
* Mechanism: The comment section processes raw strings through system shells (e.g., converting a text comment into an automated PDF receipt via a shell utility). An attacker submits a comment containing shell metacharacters like ; rm -rf /.
   * Average Payout: $8,000 – $25,000
   * Maximum Cap: $101,010 (Google VRP) [Google Applications, Google VRP rules] [1, 2] 
* 2. SQL Injection inside Comment Fields (CWE-89)
* Mechanism: The application saves comments directly into a relational database using raw text concatenation instead of parameterized queries. The attacker passes a comment containing SQL operators like ' OR 1=1;-- to read private platform data.
   * Average Payout: $5,000 – $15,000
   * Maximum Cap: $75,000+ (Google Applications) [Google Applications] [3, 4, 5, 6, 7] 
* 3. Server-Side Template Injection via Comments (SSTI / CWE-1336)
* Mechanism: The commenting system utilizes web templates (like Jinja2, Twig, or FreeMarker) to dynamically render usernames or markdown formatting, executing server-side expressions embedded in the text like {{7*7}}.
   * Average Payout: $6,500 – $20,000 [8] 
* 4. NoSQL Injection in Document Comments (CWE-943)
* Mechanism: Comment parameters are evaluated directly into unvalidated JSON queries within document databases like MongoDB, allowing attackers to dump other users' private comments.
   * Average Payout: $2,500 – $8,000 [9] 

