## Client-Side Execution & Injection Exploits (Medium to High Impact)
These flaws trigger when the application renders comments on a web page or dashboard, allowing malicious scripts inside the comment text to execute inside a victim's browser session. [10, 11] 

* 5. Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Comments (Stored XSS / CWE-79)
* Mechanism: The classic comment vulnerability. An attacker posts a comment containing HTML or JavaScript (e.g., <script>alert(document.cookie)</script>). The system saves it, and the script executes whenever any other user views that comment page.
   * Average Payout: $2,000 – $6,000 [12, 13, 14, 15, 16] 
* 6. HTML / Markdown Injection in Comments (CWE-74)
* Mechanism: The comment box allows rich formatting but fails to strip harmful HTML structural tags. Attackers inject spoofed login forms or deface the page, though it lacks direct JavaScript execution.
   * Average Payout: $200 – $1,000 [17, 18] 
* 7. CSV / Formula Injection via Comment Exports (CWE-1236)
* Mechanism: Administrators export user comments into a .csv or .xlsx spreadsheet file for auditing. An attacker submits a comment starting with math symbols, such as =CMD('cmd.exe /c calc.exe')!A1. When opened in Excel, the spreadsheet executes the command locally on the admin's machine.
   * Average Payout: $500 – $2,000

