## Logical Processing & Business Logic Exploits (High to Critical Impact)
These flaws focus on manipulating the metadata, authorization state, or processing loops tied to the comment engine.

* 8. Insecure Direct Object Reference on Comments (IDOR / CWE-639)
* Mechanism: When a user edits or deletes their comment, the system relies on an easily guessed numeric ID parameter (e.g., POST /comment/delete?id=4553). Changing the number allows the attacker to delete or modify comments written by other users or site administrators.
   * Average Payout: $3,000 – $12,000 [19, 20, 21] 
* 9. Comment Parameter Pollution / Mass Assignment (CWE-915)
* Mechanism: The backend automatically binds all submitted HTTP comment fields directly to a database object. An attacker injects hidden variables alongside their text, such as &is_pinned=true or &approved=true, to auto-approve their own spam or force it to the top of the page.
   * Average Payout: $1,000 – $4,500 [22, 23, 24] 
* 10. Incomplete Comment Sanitation Re-Dos / Algorithmic Complexity (CWE-1333)
* Mechanism: The server uses poorly written, unoptimized Regular Expressions (RegEx) to check comments for profanity or spam links. An attacker posts a highly specific, repeating string that forces the RegEx engine into an infinite evaluation loop, locking up the server CPU and causing a Denial of Service (DoS).
   * Average Payout: $500 – $3,000 [25] 

